    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d247-a2">

                <pb facs="0641" n="555" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v1/pg555"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.—Translation.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic"><persName type="from">Soyeshmia Tane-omi</persName> to
                        Mr. <persName corresp="#p_CEDL1" type="to">De Long</persName>.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office, Tokei</hi></placeName>,<lb/><date
                            calendar="japanese-nengō gregorian" when="1872-10-05"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">The</hi> 5<hi rendition="#italic">th of</hi>
                                10<hi rendition="#italic">th month of</hi> 5<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >th year of Meigi</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In reply to your excellency’s note of October 20, 1872, regarding the Lew Chew
                    Islands, I beg to state as follows:</p>
                <p>The Lew Chew Islands have been dependencies of this empire for hundreds of years,
                    and to them the title of Han was recently given.</p>
                <p>As you say, the Lew Chew being an integral portion of the Japanese Empire it is
                    natural that the provisions of a compact entered into between the Lew Chew and
                    the United States on the 11th of July, 1854, will be observed by this
                    government.</p>
                <p>With respect and consideration,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><persName>SOYESHIMA TANE-OMI</persName>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
